  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT PRESENTATION",
      "text": "Patient David Miller was triaged in the emergency department following acute onset of lightheadedness and palpitations earlier this morning. Initial hemodynamic parameters were reviewed and patient was placed on telemetry."
    },
    {
      "title": "HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS",
      "text": "The patient describes experiencing recurrent lightheadedness over the preceding 48 hours without syncope. Initial emergency workup revealed that initial platelet count was measured at 245.5 10*3/uL. Point-of-care laboratory screening additionally noted potassium resulted at 6.03 mmol/L (HIGH). Given these findings, the patient was transitioned to the Clinical Decision Unit for 24-hour observation."
    },
    {
      "title": "OBSERVATION UNIT CLINICAL COURSE",
      "text": "Throughout the observation unit stay, continuous telemetry showed normal sinus rhythm without pause or tachyarrhythmia. The patient was administered Ciprofloxacin at a dose of 250 mg PO every 12 hours for 7 days, which promptly alleviated acute symptoms. Follow-up protocol monitoring of hemoglobin demonstrated 13.5 g/dL. Orthostatic blood pressures were negative upon ambulation."
    },
    {
      "title": "DISCHARGE REGIMEN",
      "text": "Patient was deemed safe for outpatient discharge. The ongoing regimen includes Metoprolol 25 mg oral twice daily as prescribed. Prescription counseling was performed by nursing prior to release."
    },
    {
      "title": "PLAN & PRECAUTIONS",
      "text": "Resume normal daily activities as tolerated. Avoid strenuous exercise until cardiology follow-up in 1 to 2 weeks. Immediate emergency precautions detailed."
    }
  ],
